{
  // Tier-0 dead-code detection (standards/CODEBASE-CRAWLERS D1). `npm run crawl:tier0`.
  //
  // Config shape ported from life-therapy's knip.jsonc rather than derived from scratch — their
  // entry list, the `ignoreExportsUsedInFile` split and the reasons-are-mandatory rule below were
  // paid for in three cleanup passes and two broken builds. What is NOT ported is their ignore
  // list: theirs is about shadcn and Prisma, neither of which exists here.
  //
  // IN `npm run check` SINCE 2026-08-21, on the exit condition this header set: the tree is at zero.
  // It took a 103 → 0 burn-down. 56 findings were genuinely dead and were deleted, un-exported or
  // dropped from a barrel; the remaining 47 are DECISIONS, and each one now carries its reason —
  // 40 as an `@knipignore` tag at the site, 4 as file entries below, 3 as the `duplicates` rule.
  //
  // ⚠ READ THIS BEFORE ADDING AN IGNORE. Zero here means "every finding has been classified", NOT
  // "there is no dead code". The gate's aperture is narrower than it looks: `ignoreExportsUsedInFile`
  // below suppresses 174 findings that nobody will ever be shown again, and that is a deliberate
  // trade, not an oversight. Reaching zero by SILENCING is the failure mode this file exists to
  // prevent — an entry earns its place by carrying a reason a stranger can check, and the reasons
  // are the artefact, not the count.
  //
  // JSONC, not JSON, because every ignore needs its REASON. An ignore list without reasons is a
  // silencer; with them it is a decision log, and the next reader can tell a deliberate retention
  // from an oversight. Same rule this repo's eslint baselines follow.
  "$schema": "https://unpkg.com/knip@6/schema.json",

  "entry": [
    // Next.js resolves these by CONVENTION, never by import. Without them listed, every page reads
    // as an unused file and the first run is ~100% noise — which is how a tool gets run once and
    // never again.
    "app/**/{page,layout,loading,error,not-found,route,template,default,global-error}.{ts,tsx}",
    // REMOVED on the first run, all reported by knip's own configuration hints:
    //   app/**/sitemap.ts, app/**/robots.ts, middleware.ts — no such file exists here
    //   instrumentation.ts, vitest.config.ts, vitest.db.config.ts — knip resolves these by
    //     convention, so listing them is redundant
    // An entry pattern matching nothing is the same disease as a stale allowlist entry: it reads
    // as a considered decision and silently covers whatever is written at that path next. Ported
    // from LT's file, where the same six-line cleanup is recorded for the same reason.

    // Scripts are not part of the build, so anything only THEY import reads as dead. LT deleted a
    // helper on that report and broke a script silently; both extensions are listed here from the
    // start rather than after the same lesson.
    "scripts/**/*.{mjs,mts,ts}",
    "eslint-rules/**/*.mjs",

    // Tests and their harness. A helper used only by tests is not dead code.
    "**/*.{test,dbtest,spec}.{ts,tsx}",
    "test/**/*.ts",

    // The service worker. Built by serwist via next.config.ts, never imported — so without this
    // entry `serwist` reported as an unused dependency. Fixed HERE rather than in
    // ignoreDependencies: a missing entry point is a config gap, and suppressing its symptom would
    // have hidden anything else app/sw.ts alone depends on.
    "app/sw.ts"
  ],

  // ⚠ hooks/, types/ and content/ WERE MISSING, and the cost was not "knip says nothing about
  // them" — it was WORSE. A file outside `project` is invisible, so every symbol it imports reads
  // as an unused export: `hooks/useMyTeams.ts` is imported by two dashboard clients, and
  // `getMyTeams` was reported dead because the only file using it was not being analysed. Acting on
  // that report would have deleted live code. Found by a codemod over the export findings failing
  // typecheck — the report was contaminated, not the transform.
  "project": [
    "app/**/*.{ts,tsx}",
    "lib/**/*.{ts,tsx}",
    "components/**/*.{ts,tsx}",
    "hooks/**/*.{ts,tsx}",
    // `types/` DOES NOT EXIST as at 2026-08-21, so knip emits a "Refine project pattern (no
    // matches)" hint on every run. That hint is EXPECTED — do not chase it, and do not delete this
    // line to silence it. A `project` pattern that matches nothing is defensive (it costs a hint);
    // a `project` path that is MISSING when the directory appears is contaminating (it makes live
    // code read as dead, which is the mistake recorded in the comment above). The asymmetry is
    // the whole reason this stays.
    "types/**/*.ts",
    "content/**/*.{ts,tsx}",
    "scripts/**/*.{ts,mts,mjs}",
    "eslint-rules/**/*.mjs",
    "test/**/*.ts"
  ],

  // A TYPE used inside its own module is not dead code — it is the module's own vocabulary, and it
  // is usually named in an exported signature where a reader needs to be able to say the name.
  //
  // Scoped to types and interfaces DELIBERATELY: a FUNCTION used only inside its own module still
  // gets reported, because that one really should lose its `export`. LT records that conflating the
  // two broke their build three times in one pass.
  "ignoreExportsUsedInFile": { "interface": true, "type": true },

  // Deliberate keeps are marked `@knipignore <reason>` on the declaration itself. WITHOUT this key
  // the tag is inert — probed both directions 2026-08-21: no key → the tagged export is still
  // reported; key present → it clears. A site tag beats a central list here because the reason has
  // to survive next to the code, and because a stranger reading the export sees why it has no
  // caller. It does NOT work for a whole unused FILE (also probed — a file whose every export is
  // tagged still reports), which is why the four below are path entries instead.
  "tags": ["-knipignore"],

  "rules": {
    // Two names bound to one value. All three instances are deliberate and LIVE, so this reports a
    // style opinion rather than dead code: `encryptDob`/`decryptDob` alias the id_number functions
    // (9+ call sites — DOB is ciphertext on `applications`, whose column was widened date→text, and
    // calling `encryptDob` at a dob column is what makes that legible), and
    // `STEP_DOCS_OPTIONAL`/`LAST_DATA_STEP` are separately imported under both names.
    "duplicates": "off"
  },

  "ignore": [
    // ─── Unwired feature halves. Four files, two pairs, both KEPT by ruling on 2026-08-21. ───
    // These are not obsolete code; they are the half of a feature that was built first. The census
    // that found them produced two findings in the process, recorded in docs/DEAD-CODE-QUEUE.md.

    // The single-payment chain. `recordPayment` is the ONLY single-payment recording implementation
    // (bulk import calls `record_payment_atomic` directly), and its one caller is itself rendered by
    // nothing. `.claude/crawlers/INTENTIONAL.md` already holds this as an open PRODUCT question —
    // deleting the chain decides it, which is not a dead-code pass's call to make.
    "app/(dashboard)/leases/[leaseId]/QuickPaymentButton.tsx",
    "lib/actions/payments.ts",
    "lib/comms/templates/tenant/rent/payment-received.tsx",

    // The tier-downgrade pair: each file's comment names the other as the missing half
    // (`canDowngradeTo.ts` — "call buildSubscriptionForm ... once that action is built"). It
    // enforces the lease-cap invariant in the "coming down" direction, the half `canActivateLease`
    // does not cover. `buildSubscriptionForm` is tagged at its site; this one is a whole file.
    "lib/tier/canDowngradeTo.ts",

    // shadcn/ui primitives. They arrive as a SET — you install a component and its Radix dependency
    // together — and several are staged ahead of the screens that will use them. Reporting them
    // every run trains people to skim the whole report, which costs more than the few unused files.
    // Same call life-therapy made, for the same reason.
    //
    // This is the entry most likely to rot into cover for real dead code, and the trade is
    // deliberate: components/ui/ is vendored, near-never hand-edited, and its contents are
    // traceable to `npx shadcn add`. Currently covering avatar, dropdown-menu, scroll-area,
    // separator and table.
    "components/ui/**",

    // VERSIONED PROMPTS ARE AN AUDIT RECORD, NOT DEAD CODE — and this is the entry that would have
    // done real damage. Only the CURRENT version is imported, so knip reports every superseded one
    // as unused. But `fitscore_narrative_prompt_version` is STORED on the decision row
    // (`narr.v1.0` appears in fitScoreReplay.test.ts's fixtures), and replay's whole purpose is
    // answering "what was in force on the decision date?" for a tribunal. Deleting a superseded
    // prompt deletes the answer.
    //
    // The exit condition is unusual for this file: these leave when the DECISIONS naming them fall
    // out of retention, never when the code stops importing them.
    "lib/screening/prompts/**",

    // CONSUMED BY PATH, NOT BY IMPORT — the class an import graph cannot see, and neither knip nor
    // a hand-rolled resolver would have caught it. `scripts/check-marketing-consistency.mjs` (in
    // `npm run check`) does `readFile(join(ROOT, "lib", "marketing", "canonical-phrases.ts"))` and
    // parses the string literals out of it as its SSOT. Nothing imports the module, so every
    // import-based analysis calls it dead; deleting it turns the marketing gate red with no import
    // anywhere to explain why.
    "lib/marketing/canonical-phrases.ts"
  ],

  // Each entry below was read, classified, and VERIFIED BY REMOVAL — taken out, knip re-run, the
  // finding reappeared. An ignore verified by reading is a guess; LT records the same rule after
  // finding five of theirs suppressed nothing at all.
  //
  // What is NOT here is as deliberate: eight packages knip called unused really were unused and
  // were UNINSTALLED rather than ignored — @hookform/resolvers, react-hook-form (a pair, nothing
  // imported either), @tiptap/extension-link (the editor imports extension-UNDERLINE, which was
  // not declared at all), heic2any, pdf-lib, zustand, react-email (the preview CLI; the app uses
  // @react-email/components, a different package) and @types/pdf-parse (v1 types against
  // pdf-parse v2, which ships its own).
  "ignoreDependencies": [
    // Reached through CSS, which knip does not follow — its own configuration hint says so
    // (".css — Compiled extension excluded by project (imports not followed)"). Both are imported
    // by app/globals.css; deleting either breaks every page's styling with no import to show it.
    "tw-animate-css",
    "tailwindcss",

    // Named as strings in .releaserc.json and loaded by semantic-release at release time. No
    // source file imports them, and the release job is the only thing that resolves them.
    "@semantic-release/commit-analyzer",
    "@semantic-release/release-notes-generator",
    "@semantic-release/github",
    // One level further out: .releaserc.json says `"preset": "conventionalcommits"`, which
    // semantic-release resolves BY NAME to this package. Nothing anywhere spells the package name.
    "conventional-changelog-conventionalcommits",

    // CLI tools invoked by hand, never imported. `shadcn` is configured by components.json;
    // `supabase` is the local-stack CLI the DB test tier drives.
    "shadcn",
    "supabase"
  ]
}
